@database "coder"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: coder.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Coder"
@{h1}Coder

A coding agent with a harness around it. @{b}coder@{ub} plans a change,
edits the files, runs the tests and has the finished change attacked by a
reviewer before it calls the work done. Three sub-agents do the parts that
would cost it its own context or its own bias: @{b}coder_explorer@{ub}
finds things, @{b}coder_reviewer@{ub} reviews read-only,
@{b}coder_tester@{ub} runs the checks. What it learns about a project it
keeps in a knowledge bundle that is folded into later sessions.

The plugin is configuration only: four agents, their prompts, five tool
instances and two skills. It has no tools, hooks or panel of its own.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Working with it  " link use}
  @{"  The four agents  " link agents}
  @{"  What it remembers  " link knowledge}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Calling it from another agent  " link tool}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node use "Working with it"
@{h1}Working with it

In the web UI, pick @{b}coder@{ub} as the agent of a chat (see
@{" Choosing an agent " link scarabhive/agents}). From the command line:

@{code sh}
agent-cli chat --agent coder
agent-cli run --agent coder "add a --dry-run flag to tools/sync.py"
@{body}

All four agents are gated at @{b}admin@{ub}: they have a shell and read
the whole checkout, @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} included, so only an
admin account (or the local operator of agent-cli) can start them.

@{h2}What it reaches

The file tools reach @{tt}data/workspace/@{utt}, @{tt}src/@{utt} and the
repository root -- the coder works on this project itself, and can change
its own configuration. The shell starts in the repository root, so one
relative path names the same file in both. The shell is not sandboxed: it
runs what an admin could run there, and its blacklist (@{tt}rm -rf /@{utt},
@{tt}git push@{utt}, @{tt}git reset --hard@{utt}, @{tt}git checkout --@{utt},
@{tt}git clean -@{utt} and a few more) is a guard against typos, not a
boundary (see @{" What an agent can run " link terminal/security}).

@{h2}What a report looks like

The coder closes every answer with three lines:

@{code}
Ran:      <what it executed, and what came back>
Check:    <the test or self-check it left behind, or why none is needed>
Reviewed: <the coder_reviewer round and its findings, or "skipped: <reason>">
@{body}

A skipped review is allowed; an unmentioned one is not. When it cannot
finish, it says where it stopped and what is missing.

@{h2}Commits and git

It never commits or pushes on its own. Asked to commit, it commits only
the files it changed, by path. It first checks with
@{tt}git rev-parse --show-toplevel@{utt} which repository git is talking
about: a project under @{tt}data/workspace/@{utt} is ignored by this
repository, so there git shows other people's changes and none of its own
until the project is a repository of its own.

@{h2}Undo

Every file @{tt}coder_fs@{utt} writes is recorded:
@{tt}/undo files@{utt} and @{tt}/rewind@{utt} put the files back (see
@{" Undo and rewind in the chat " link file_checkpoints/using}). What the
shell changed is only named, not recorded.

@{h2}Claude Code and GitLab/GitHub

Where Claude Code is installed (@{" Coding CLI " link coding_cli/main}),
the coder can hand a self-contained task to it; the result comes back on a
branch, and the coder reviews it like its own work before applying it.
Where repositories are configured for @{" Forge " link forge/main}, it
works tickets from issue to merge. Neither is there otherwise, and the
prompt then does not mention them.

@{h2}Limits

@{table}
Limit | Value
Steps per run | coder 300, reviewer 200, explorer and tester 40
Context window | 200,000 tokens, all four
Answer cut off at the output cap | sent back with a note, up to twice in a row (coder only)
Sub-agents | 4 per type not archived (a fifth archives the oldest), 20 per session
Wait for a sub-agent | 3600 s
@{body}
@endnode

@node agents "The four agents"
@{h1}The four agents

@{table}
Agent | Does | Tools | Models
coder | the change: plans, edits, runs, gets reviewed | everything below, writable files | code, code_fallback
coder_explorer | "where is X, who calls Y" -- paths and lines, no judgement | read-only files | structured, structured_fallback
coder_reviewer | attacks a finished change, reports only findings that survived a refutation attempt | read-only files, skills, sequential_thinking | code, code_fallback
coder_tester | runs tests, lint, types; reports only the failing lines; mutation probe | shell, read-only files | structured, structured_fallback
@{body}

The model names are profiles of the installation's LLM configuration
(see @{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}). The coder and
the reviewer escalate to @{tt}code_adv@{utt}, @{tt}code_adv_fallback@{utt},
the tester to @{tt}code@{utt}, @{tt}code_fallback@{utt}; the explorer has
no escalation chain.

@{h2}Why separate agents

@{bullet}@{b}Context@{ub}: sweeping thirty files to answer one question
costs the explorer its context, not the coder's. The same holds for the
hundreds of lines of a failing test run.
@{bullet}@{b}Fresh eyes@{ub}: the reviewer sees none of the coder's
conversation, only what the coder hands it.
@{bullet}@{b}Read-only by configuration@{ub}: explorer and reviewer get
@{tt}coder_fs_ro@{utt}, a file_ops instance with @{tt}read_only: true@{utt};
it offers no write tools at all. The tester has no file write tools
either, but its shell can write -- the mutation probe needs that.

None of the three has a sub-agent manager, so a review cannot start a
review. The coder starts them through @{tt}coder_sam@{utt}, which allows
exactly these three.
@endnode

@node knowledge "What it remembers"
@{h1}What it remembers

@{h2}The knowledge bundle

@{tt}data/okf/coder@{utt} is an OKF bundle: markdown concepts with YAML
front matter and links between them. The coder writes what a later
session would otherwise rediscover the hard way -- a measured fact with
its conditions, a trap with its evidence, a convention no file states, a
decision with its reason -- and never what the code already says. The
directory is created on the first write.

Before each LLM call a hook picks the three concepts that best match the
last message a person wrote, adds the concepts they link to, and appends
at most ten of them as one turn. Each body is cut at 1,500 characters
with @{tt}…[truncated]@{utt}; the skill therefore asks for concepts under
that length. A retired concept is marked as such. The block is appended
again only when the selection changed, which within one turn it does not
-- unless the coder wrote a concept.

The bundle has its own OKF instance, @{tt}coder_okf@{utt}, sandboxed to
this one directory: the coder cannot write into another agent's bundle.

@{h2}The skills

@{table}
Skill | Where it comes from | In the prompt
coding-harness | this plugin | the coder's, in full: the working loop, when to delegate, what to carry forward, the report contract
adversarial-review | this plugin | the reviewer's, in full; for the coder only its description
tdd, diagnosing-bugs, codebase-design | @{tt}skills/coding/@{utt} | the coder's (and codebase-design the reviewer's), description only
forge-workflow | the forge plugin | the coder's, description only
@{body}

The coder can edit the two skills of this plugin: they lie inside its
sandbox.

@{h2}The project's own instructions

All four agents read the project's @{tt}AGENTS.md@{utt} from the
repository root and keep that version for the session; see
@{" Project Instructions " link project_instructions/main}.
@endnode

@node tool "Calling it from another agent"
@{h1}Calling it from another agent

The coder is visible in the UI and as a tool (@{tt}visibility: both@{utt});
the three sub-agents only as tools. No shipped agent calls them as a tool;
to let one, put @{tt}"+coder/*"@{utt} (or @{tt}"+coder_reviewer/*"@{utt} ...) into
its @{tt}agent_config.tools.allowed@{utt}. The caller then has
@{tt}coder_execute_task@{utt} and @{tt}coder_list_available_tools@{utt};
see @{" execute_task " link basic_agent/execute_task}.
@{tt}use_advanced_model@{utt} switches to the escalation chain named in
@{" The four agents " link agents}. The admin gate applies to every way in.

The plugin sets its own one-line descriptions for these tools, e.g. for
@{tt}coder_execute_task@{utt}: "Implement, fix or refactor code in the
workspace -- plans the change, edits files, runs the tests and has the
result reviewed before reporting".

The @{b}gamedev@{ub} agent of the Godot plugin inherits the coder
(@{tt}type: coder@{utt}): its sandbox and shell, with its own prompt,
skills, bundle and sub-agent manager. Its manager starts @{tt}coder_explorer@{utt} and
@{tt}coder_reviewer@{utt} as well, and the Godot plugin's
@{tt}godot_agent@{utt} uses @{tt}coder_fs@{utt}: a change to the
instances or the roles here changes them too.

@{h2}As sub-agents

Another agent's manager can start @{tt}coder_explorer@{utt},
@{tt}coder_reviewer@{utt} or @{tt}coder_tester@{utt} when they are in its
@{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}; see
@{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link scarabhive/subagents}. Give them
everything in the task: they see nothing of the caller's conversation.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The prompts

Read from @{tt}agents/prompts/@{utt} on every run. Rendered sizes:

@{table}
Agent | Characters | Contents
coder | about 13,800; 16,000 with Claude Code and forge | sandbox, shell and commit rules, deferred tools, the bundle, the sub-agent table, the done bar, then the coding-harness skill and the index of five on-demand skills
coder_explorer | about 2,600 | how to search (grep first, semantic search for an unknown word), what to report
coder_reviewer | about 7,400 | what counts as a finding, the report format, then the adversarial-review skill and the codebase-design index
coder_tester | about 2,700 | run what was asked, report failures verbatim, the mutation probe
@{body}

The coder's sections on Claude Code and on GitLab/GitHub are rendered
only when @{tt}has_tool('coding_cli_run_task')@{utt} or
@{tt}has_tool('forge_checkout')@{utt} finds the tool. The bundle path is
filled in from @{tt}template_vars.okf_bundle@{utt}. No prompt renders a
value that changes between calls.

@{h2}The coder's tools

@{table}
Tool | From
coder_fs_read_file, _list_directory, _search_files, _grep_search, _semantic_search, _replace_string_in_file, _manage | @{" file_ops " link file_ops/main}, instance @{tt}coder_fs@{utt}
coder_shell_execute, _get_output, _kill_process | @{" terminal " link terminal/main}, instance @{tt}coder_shell@{utt}
coder_sam_manage_sub_agent | the sub-agent manager, instance @{tt}coder_sam@{utt}
skills_list, skills_read | @{" skills " link skills/main}
todo | @{" todo " link todo/main}
ask_user | @{" ask_user " link ask_user/main}
context_engineer_list, _read, _store_fact, _compact | @{" context_engineer " link context_engineer/main}
coder_okf_search, _read_concept, _neighbors, _subgraph, _list, _write_concept, _append_log, _validate, _reindex | deferred; OKF, instance @{tt}coder_okf@{utt}
sequential_thinking and its two helpers, datetime_operations | deferred
coding_cli_run_task, _get_run, _cancel_run | deferred; only where Claude Code is installed
forge_* | deferred; only where repositories are configured
@{body}

Deferred tools reach the model as a name and one line in the description
of @{tt}tool_search@{utt}; it loads them before the first call (see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}). The prompt names
@{tt}coder_okf@{utt} as one of them and tells the model to pass
@{tt}bundle@{utt} to every OKF call -- there is no default.

The explorer has @{tt}coder_fs_ro@{utt}'s five read tools and the
context engineer's; the reviewer adds the skills tools and
sequential_thinking; the tester has @{tt}coder_shell@{utt} and the read
tools.

@{h2}Semantic search

@{tt}coder_fs_semantic_search@{utt} answers a question in a sentence with
functions and classes, each with file, line and signature. Only
@{tt}coder_fs@{utt} builds the index, in the background, starting with
its first semantic search; @{tt}coder_fs_ro@{utt} reads the same
collection. The coder's prompt therefore tells it to ask one question
early. Until the index is there the answer says so and points to grep:

@{code}
The semantic index is being built in the background. Use grep_search now and try again in a minute.
This instance only reads the shared index 'file_ops_coder_tree', which another instance builds, and it is still empty. Use grep_search.
@{body}

See @{" Semantic search " link file_ops/semantic}.

@{h2}Hooks that run for them

@{table}
Hook | coder | sub-agents
context_engineer.engineer_context | on | on
markdown_formatter.format_markdown_output | on | off
coder_okf.okf_context_injection | on, bundle @{tt}data/okf/coder@{utt} | --
project_instructions.inject_project_instructions | on, root @{tt}.@{utt} | on
file_checkpoints.record_before_change, _after_change | on | on
todo.inject_todo_tasks, sequential_thinking.inject_active_sessions, coder_sam.inject_sub_agent_context | off | off
@{body}

The last three are off on purpose: each would append the agent's own tool
results again whenever they change, which is mid-turn, for content that is
in the transcript already. The sub-agents switch on the checkpoint hooks
although they write nothing, so the coder's checkpoint does not name them
as spawns whose changes go unrecorded.

@{h2}Prompt cache

The system prompts are fixed for a run, and all four run with
@{tt}prompt_cache_mode: multi_turn@{utt}. Injections are appended, not
inserted; what breaks the cached prefix is the context engineer's
compaction and loading a deferred tool, which appends its schema to the
tool list once.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the files are found by convention.

@{table}
File | Picked up by
agents/coder.yaml, coder_explorer.yaml, coder_reviewer.yaml, coder_tester.yaml | @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} includes @{tt}../src/plugins*/*/agents/*.yaml@{utt}
agents/tools.yaml | the same include; defines the five tool instances
agents/prompts/*.md | @{tt}system_template@{utt}, relative to the YAML
skills/coding-harness, skills/adversarial-review | @{tt}skills.skill_dirs@{utt} lists @{tt}src/plugins*/*/skills@{utt}
@{body}

@{h2}What it needs

The shared instances in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}: @{tt}skills@{utt},
@{tt}todo@{utt}, @{tt}ask_user@{utt}, @{tt}sequential_thinking@{utt},
@{tt}datetime@{utt}, @{tt}context_engineer@{utt},
@{tt}markdown_formatter@{utt}, @{tt}project_instructions@{utt},
@{tt}file_checkpoints@{utt}; optionally @{tt}coding_cli@{utt} and
@{tt}forge@{utt}. The LLM profiles @{tt}code@{utt},
@{tt}code_fallback@{utt}, @{tt}code_adv@{utt}, @{tt}code_adv_fallback@{utt},
@{tt}structured@{utt}, @{tt}structured_fallback@{utt}. On the server:
whatever the projects build and test with.

@{h2}The file sandboxes (coder_fs, coder_fs_ro)

@{table}
Key | Value
allowed_directories | data/workspace/, src/plugins/coder/skills/, src/, . -- the same on both
read_only | coder_fs_ro only
max_unpaginated_file_size_kb | 200 (default in code 100)
default_line_limit | 500
search.collection_name | file_ops_coder_tree, both
search.enable_indexing | coder_fs only; reindex every 300 s
search.exclude_patterns | .git, __pycache__, node_modules, .venv, *.pyc, *.min.js, *.min.css, *.map, dist, build
@{body}

The two directory lists must stay identical: a reviewer that cannot read
what the coder wrote reviews what it can reach and does not say so. To
keep the coder out of this repository, drop @{tt}src/@{utt} and
@{tt}.@{utt} from both. The exclude list replaces file_ops' default list,
so it repeats it. See
@{" Where an agent can read and write " link file_ops/sandbox}.

@{h2}The shell (coder_shell)

@{table}
Key | Value | Default in code
platform.initial_cwd | . | --
security.blacklist | rm -rf /, dd if=, mkfs, chmod 777, git push, git reset --hard, git checkout --, git clean - | none
security.allow_command_chains | true | true
limits.max_output_size_kb | 60 | 60
limits.default_timeout_seconds | 300 | 300
limits.max_timeout_seconds | 1800 | 3600
limits.max_concurrent_background | 5 | 10
@{body}

Blacklist entries are regular expressions, searched in the command
ignoring case.

@{h2}The bundle (coder_okf)

@{table}
Key | Value | Default in code
allowed_directories | data/okf/coder | --
hook_max_concepts | 10 | 6
hook_graph_depth | 1 | 1
max_concept_file_kb | 512 | 512
max_bundle_files | 5000 | 5000
@{body}

The hook reads these from the instance; the coder's override sets only
@{tt}hook_bundle@{utt}, without which the hook does nothing. A
@{tt}hook_bundle@{utt} outside the instance's directory also makes it do
nothing, silently.

@{h2}The sub-agent manager (coder_sam)

@{table}
Key | Value | Default in code
allowed_agents | coder_explorer, coder_reviewer, coder_tester | --
max_nesting_depth | 2 | 5
max_sub_agents_per_type | 4 | 3
max_sub_agents_per_session | 20 | 10
default_wait_timeout | 3600 | 3600
auto_archive_on_limit | true | false
@{body}

A new role needs its YAML under @{tt}agents/@{utt} and an entry in
@{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}, or it cannot be started.

@{h2}Tests

@{tt}tests/test_coder_harness.py@{utt} checks the configuration as it
resolves: the read-only instance renders no write tools, both sandboxes
cover the same tree and share one index with one builder, the exclude
lists keep file_ops' defaults, every tool a prompt names exists and is
allowed, the Claude Code section appears exactly when the tool does, the
bundle is isolated and reachable by the hook, the churning hooks stay
off, and every agent reads @{tt}AGENTS.md@{utt}.
@endnode
